---
import BaseHead from "../components/BaseHead.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import ColorScript from "../components/ColorScript.astro";
import { SITE_TITLE } from "../config";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-us">
  <BaseHead
    title={`About · ${SITE_TITLE}`}
    description="AI Engineer at IBM — building production LLM systems, RAG pipelines, and intelligent agents for enterprise operations."
  />
  <body>
    <Header title={SITE_TITLE} />
    <main id="main">
      <article class="about-page">

        <header class="page-header">
          <div class="page-label">// about</div>
          <h1>Syed Asad Zaman</h1>
          <p class="subtitle">Computer Scientist · IBM · Islamabad, Pakistan</p>
        </header>

        <section class="intro-block">
          <p>
            I'm a computer scientist first — the specific problem has changed more than once:
            reinforcement learning, differential privacy, data engineering, and for the last two
            years, production AI. What stays the same is the approach: read the actual system, find
            what's actually broken, and build the smallest thing that fixes it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Lately that's meant <strong>DB HealthWatch</strong> — a GenAI assistant that turns hours
            of AWR reports and log correlation into a diagnosis in minutes — alongside two other
            production systems I've shipped at IBM: patient engagement and customer support automation.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="section-block">
          <h2>How I Think</h2>
          <p>
            Whatever the problem, I want to understand the actual system before I touch it — the
            schema nobody documented, the workaround someone added under deadline, the assumption
            baked into data that stopped being true two years ago. Reinforcement learning,
            differential privacy, AI systems — different domains, same first move.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three production AI systems taught me where the real engineering is: not in choosing the
            model, but in everything around it — context management, tool design, failure modes, cost
            at scale, what happens when the system is confidently wrong. Right now that's applied to
            IBM's enterprise asset data: decades of operational records, inconsistent schemas,
            business logic that lives in one person's head and in no document.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="section-block">
          <h2>Experience</h2>
          <div class="timeline">

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <div class="timeline-header">
                <span class="timeline-org">IBM — AI Engineer</span>
                <span class="timeline-date">Oct 2024 – Present</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Building GenAI-powered tools for enterprise environments.
                Shipped <strong>DB HealthWatch</strong> (LLM-driven database diagnostics —
                60% faster resolution, 35% faster incident response) and
                <strong>MediCare.AI</strong> (multi-channel patient engagement on WhatsApp,
                Telegram, and Instagram, running 24/7). Both in production, both handling
                real workloads.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <div class="timeline-header">
                <span class="timeline-org">IBM — Business Transformation Consultant</span>
                <span class="timeline-date">May 2024 – Present</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                National-scale IBM Maximo Application Suite deployment for Irish Water —
                Ireland's national utility. 1.9M+ assets, 3.4M+ locations, 1.2M+ work orders.
                Built 25 automation scripts and integrations across GIS, SCADA, and ERP.
                This is where I see firsthand how much value is trapped in operational data
                that no system can reason over yet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <div class="timeline-header">
                <span class="timeline-org">IBM — Package Consultant</span>
                <span class="timeline-date">Sep 2023 – May 2024</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Level 2 AMS for Vodafone's CVM ecosystem — DataStage, Unica Suite, Oracle DB,
                middleware. Incidents, deployments, upgrades under strict SLAs for a Tier-1
                telecom operator. Where I learned that real systems are messier, older, and
                more fragile than any tutorial suggests.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <div class="timeline-header">
                <span class="timeline-org">Giggso — Trainee ML Engineer</span>
                <span class="timeline-date">Aug 2022 – Nov 2022</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Data observability platform using Soda Core, Airflow, and Great Expectations.
                My first real exposure to the gap between academic ML and production data engineering.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <div class="timeline-header">
                <span class="timeline-org">COLAB @ FAST NUCES — Research Group Lead</span>
                <span class="timeline-date">Jun 2021 – Jul 2022</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Led a 30-person research team under Dr. Mohammad Nauman. Weekly presentations
                to 100+ students. Forced me to get good at explaining complex ideas simply —
                a skill I use more than any technical one.
                <a href="/posts/how-colab-transformed-my-journey">Read the full story →</a>
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="timeline-item">
              <div class="timeline-header">
                <span class="timeline-org">FAST NUCES — BS Computer Science</span>
                <span class="timeline-date">2018 – 2022</span>
              </div>
              <p>
                Final year project: <strong>HyperDrive</strong> — autonomous navigation using
                Deep Reinforcement Learning in CARLA (DQN, DDQN, D* Search).
                Research: Attribute Division using Three Way Decisions for Differential Privacy
                (ORIC funded). Crashed into virtual walls. Learned how to debug RL agents.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section-block">
          <h2>Opinions</h2>
          <div class="beliefs">
            <div class="belief">
              <span class="belief-label">on RAG vs fine-tuning</span>
              <p>
                For 90% of enterprise use cases, RAG wins. It's cheaper, faster to iterate,
                and doesn't require retraining when your data changes. Fine-tuning is powerful,
                but most companies reach for it before they've exhausted what RAG can do.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="belief">
              <span class="belief-label">on agents</span>
              <p>
                Most "agent" failures are context management failures dressed up in LangGraph.
                Before you add another agent to your pipeline, fix your tool design and task boundaries.
                Complexity distributed across more agents is still complexity.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="belief">
              <span class="belief-label">on shipping</span>
              <p>
                A working system that solves 80% of the problem is worth more than a perfect
                system in a notebook. Production teaches humility faster than any benchmark.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="belief">
              <span class="belief-label">on AI in enterprise</span>
              <p>
                The bottleneck isn't the model. It's the 30-year-old dataset nobody cleaned,
                the workflow nobody documented, and the SME who retires next year.
                AI doesn't fix bad data. It amplifies it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="section-block">
          <h2>Stack</h2>
          <div class="skills-table">
            <div class="skill-row">
              <span class="skill-cat">LLM / AI</span>
              <span class="skill-val">LangChain · LangGraph · RAG (FAISS, Pinecone) · Prompt Engineering · OpenAI GPT · IBM watsonx.ai · Hugging Face</span>
            </div>
            <div class="skill-row">
              <span class="skill-cat">Backend</span>
              <span class="skill-val">Python · FastAPI · Streamlit · SQL · JavaScript · Java · Jython</span>
            </div>
            <div class="skill-row">
              <span class="skill-cat">Databases</span>
              <span class="skill-val">Oracle · PostgreSQL · MongoDB · Pinecone · FAISS · Supabase</span>
            </div>
            <div class="skill-row">
              <span class="skill-cat">In production</span>
              <span class="skill-val">3 LLM systems · 10K+ daily queries · healthcare, telecom, utilities</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section class="contact-block">
          <h2>Work with me</h2>
          <p>
            I'm open to consulting on AI system design, RAG architecture, and LLM integration
            for enterprise use cases — especially where there's real operational data and a
            genuine problem to solve.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also happy to talk shop, debate RAG vs fine-tuning, or hear about problems
            you haven't figured out yet.
          </p>
          <div class="contact-links">
            <a href="mailto:essayyzed@gmail.com">essayyzed@gmail.com</a>
            <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/essayyzed/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">linkedin</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/essayyzed" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github</a>
            <a href="https://twitter.com/essayyzed" target="_blank" rel="noopener">twitter</a>
          </div>
        </section>

      </article>
    </main>
    <Footer />
    <ColorScript />
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  .about-page { max-width: 68ch; }

  .page-header { margin: 2rem 0 2rem; }
  .page-label {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--fg-subtle);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .page-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 4vw, 2.6rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
  }
  .subtitle { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--fg-muted); }

  /* Intro */
  .intro-block {
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--bg-alt);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;
    margin: 0 0 2rem;
  }
  .intro-block p { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: 1.8; color: var(--fg-muted); }
  .intro-block p + p { margin-top: 1em; }

  /* Sections */
  .section-block { margin: 2rem 0; }
  .section-block h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--fg-subtle);
    margin-bottom: 1.1rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .section-block > p { color: var(--fg-muted); line-height: 1.8; font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  .section-block > p + p { margin-top: 0.9em; }

  /* Timeline */
  .timeline { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .timeline-item {
    padding: 1.1rem 0 1.1rem 1.25rem;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--border);
    position: relative;
  }
  .timeline-item::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: -5px; top: 1.5rem;
    width: 8px; height: 8px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--accent);
    border: 2px solid var(--bg);
  }
  .timeline-header {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: baseline;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.2rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
  }
  .timeline-org { font-weight: 600; font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--fg); }
  .timeline-date { font-size: var(--text-xs); color: var(--fg-subtle); }
  .timeline-item p {
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    line-height: 1.75;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    margin: 0;
  }
  .timeline-item p + p { margin-top: 0.6em; }
  .timeline-item a { font-size: inherit; }

  /* Opinions */
  .beliefs { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .belief { padding: 0.85rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light); }
  .belief:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .belief-label {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    color: var(--fg-subtle);
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
  }
  .belief p { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--fg-muted); line-height: 1.7; margin: 0; }

  /* Stack */
  .skills-table { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .skill-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 100px 1fr;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 0.7rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-light);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .skill-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .skill-cat {
    color: var(--fg-subtle);
    font-weight: 600;
    white-space: nowrap;
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    padding-top: 0.1rem;
  }
  .skill-val { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--fg-muted); line-height: 1.6; }

  /* Contact */
  .contact-block {
    margin: 2rem 0 1rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .contact-block h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--fg-subtle);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }
  .contact-block p { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--fg-muted); line-height: 1.7; margin: 0; }
  .contact-block p + p { margin-top: 0.6em; }
  .contact-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4rem 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .contact-links a {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color var(--transition);
  }
  .contact-links a:hover { color: var(--accent); }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .skill-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.2rem; }
    .timeline-header { flex-direction: column; }
  }
</style>
